{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786209976,"nonce":"MQ6qXDu6GlB8LIs1RNdmFz6pqJA=","title":"**What is wrong.** `deploy/helm/oto/` contains one empty directory,","message":"`templates/.gitkeep`. There is no `Chart.yaml`, no `values.yaml`, no template.\nSPEC:3428 lays out `deploy/helm/oto/{Chart.yaml,values.yaml,templates/}` and\nSPEC:3519 makes it **acceptance criterion 31** — \"`helm install oto` … is the\nentire install\". ADRs 0014 and 0015 both argue their positions (Postgres-only, no\nstream processor) on the grounds that the deployment stays a single chart.\n\n**Why it matters.** oto's pitch is \"self-hosted, without adopting an AIOps\nplatform\". A Kubernetes user who reaches for the chart the SPEC promises finds an\nempty folder and has to hand-write Deployments, a migration job, secrets for the\nkeyring and Slack, the two Postgres pools, and the River worker — which is exactly\nthe adoption cost the product exists to avoid. `README.md:74-78` now warns the\ndirectory is empty, so the docs are honest, but the criterion is unmet.\n\n**Where.** `deploy/helm/oto/`, SPEC:3428 and SPEC:3519, ADR 0014, ADR 0015,\n`README.md:74-78`, `docs/ORCHESTRATION.md:101-102`.\n\n**Done when.** `helm install oto ./deploy/helm/oto` on a clean cluster with an\nexternal Postgres URL brings up a working oto: migrations run as a pre-install\nhook, the API and worker are up, `/readyz` passes, secrets are not baked into the\nimage, and the values file covers the Slack credentials, the keyring, the two\npool sizes and ingress. Installing it is done at least once against a real (kind\nis enough) cluster, not just `helm template`.","files":null}]}